1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,835
So, here's the one
that's water cooled.

2
00:00:11,878 --> 00:00:14,314
OK, don't blink.

3
00:00:14,314 --> 00:00:19,152
Five, four, three, two, one.

4
00:00:25,125 --> 00:00:28,462
That's the speed
of sound in glass,

5
00:00:28,462 --> 00:00:30,997
which is, I think, about three
times faster than the speed

6
00:00:30,997 --> 00:00:32,866
of sound in air.

7
00:00:32,866 --> 00:00:34,468
So you can't see it.

8
00:00:34,468 --> 00:00:36,903
But there is some great
high-speed video--

9
00:00:36,903 --> 00:00:39,139
maybe some of you have
seen that online--

10
00:00:39,139 --> 00:00:41,675
of Prince Rupert
drops exploding.

11
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:43,643
And actually up in
the Edgerton Center

12
00:00:43,643 --> 00:00:46,346
they have a camera
that can catch that,

13
00:00:46,346 --> 00:00:48,849
and they've got some
really cool video.

14
00:00:48,849 --> 00:00:49,850
I'm going to leave this.

15
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:51,451
This is a very
cool fragment that

16
00:00:51,451 --> 00:00:54,721
shows the fracture, the
failure pattern, which

17
00:00:54,721 --> 00:00:55,655
is really interesting.

18
00:00:55,655 --> 00:00:58,258
It fails from the
center of the drop

19
00:00:58,258 --> 00:01:00,794
radially out at an
angle that looks

20
00:01:00,794 --> 00:01:03,296
to be about a 45-degree angle.

21
00:01:03,296 --> 00:01:04,564
You can probably explain that.

22
00:01:04,564 --> 00:01:05,932
That will be on
the quiz tomorrow.

23
00:01:05,932 --> 00:01:08,668
[LAUGHTER]

24
00:01:09,169 --> 00:01:11,037
45 degree [INAUDIBLE]?

25
00:01:11,037 --> 00:01:12,806
Was there a question?

26
00:01:12,806 --> 00:01:15,742
I'm going to do one more
just in case anybody blinked,

27
00:01:15,742 --> 00:01:19,311
and then I'm going
to be on my way.
